VT_TALK("sql/sql-scale", {
 "grow": "A single database server can do a surprising amount of work, and SQLite alone runs inside countless phone apps. When one server is no longer enough, you can scale up, buying a bigger machine, or scale out, spreading the work over many machines. Scaling out is much harder, because data must be copied or split and still stay correct. The sensible order is to fix queries and indexes first, then cache repeated reads, then add read replicas, then partition big tables, and only then shard across servers. Most apps never need the last step. Think of a busy restaurant that first speeds up its kitchen before opening a second branch.",
 "partition": "Partitioning splits one huge table into smaller pieces on the same server, using a rule such as the year of each sale. When a query asks for 2025, the engine reads only the 2025 piece and skips the rest, which is called partition pruning. Dropping a whole old year becomes instant, instead of deleting millions of rows one by one. Pieces can follow ranges such as months, lists such as countries, or a hash that spreads keys evenly. PostgreSQL has this built in, and in SQLite you can imitate it with one table per year behind a UNION ALL view. Think of a filing cabinet with one drawer per year.",
 "replicate": "Replication keeps full copies of the database on other servers. The primary takes every write and streams its change log to replicas, which answer read queries and can take over if the primary fails. With asynchronous replication, a write is confirmed as soon as the primary has it, which is fast, but replicas run a little behind, and a crash can lose the newest changes. Synchronous replication waits for a replica too, so nothing is lost, but every write is slower. The delay is called replication lag, and it is why a comment you just posted can seem to vanish on reload. Think of a teacher's notes being copied by students who write a little slower.",
 "shard": "Sharding splits the rows of a table across several servers, so writes are shared out, which replication alone cannot do. The shard key decides where each row lives. A good key, such as customer id, spreads data evenly and appears in most queries. A bad key, such as today's date, sends every new write to one server. In the animation, choose a key and the number of servers, and see which servers a query must visit. The CAP theorem says that when the network between servers breaks, a database must choose between staying consistent and refusing some requests, or staying available and risking old answers. Think of a library split across several buildings.",
 "olap": "Databases do two different jobs. OLTP, online transaction processing, runs the app with many small reads and writes, like placing an order. OLAP, online analytical processing, answers big questions over years of history, like total sales by region by month. OLAP systems often store data by column instead of by row, so a query that sums one column reads only that column, and similar values compress well. Warehouses use a star schema, with one big fact table of numbers joined to small dimension tables such as date and product. Data is copied from the app database into the warehouse so reports never slow down the shop. Think of a shop's till receipts versus the yearly accounts book.",
 "levels": "Let us climb the ladder. For a beginner, when one server is too small, copy the data for reading or split it into parts. At college level, know partitioning, replication and sharding, and that OLTP and OLAP need different designs. At masters level, consensus protocols such as Raft keep copies in agreement, and distributed SQL engines combine all of these with transactions. At research level, storage is separated from compute, and table formats bring transactions to files in cloud storage. Think of a newspaper printed in many cities from the same master copy."
});
